{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_28","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/calc_small.py | sed -n '207,230p;716,746p'\"","aggregated_output":"   207\t# ---------- arithmetic ----------\n   208\tdef _result_unit(op, units):\n   209\t    if any(u not in UNITS for u in units):\n   210\t        return None\n   211\t    if op in ('sum', 'difference'):\n   212\t        return units[0] if len(set(units)) == 1 else None\n   213\t    if op == 'product':\n   214\t        money, pct = units.count('USD bn'), units.count('%')\n   215\t        if money + pct > 1:                                  # never USD bn × % (use share), never two amounts or two percentages\n   216\t            return None\n   217\t        return 'USD bn' if money else ('%' if pct else 'x')\n   218\t    if op == 'ratio':\n   219\t        a, b = units\n   220\t        return 'x' if a == b else (a if b == 'x' else None)  # [54] same units give x; a ÷ x keeps a's unit\n   221\t    if op == 'share':\n   222\t        return units[0] if units[1] == '%' else None         # [54] a share of a percentage is a percentage\n   223\t    if op == 'growth':\n   224\t        return '%' if units[0] == units[1] else None\n   225\t    if op == 'apply_growth':\n   226\t        return units[0] if units[1] == '%' else None\n   227\t    return None\n   228\t\n   229\t\n   230\tdef _apply(op, xs):\n   716\t            continue\n   717\t        years = {p[0] for p in parsed}\n   718\t        *parts_p, out = parsed\n   719\t        if len(years) != 1:\n   720\t            ok = False\n   721\t        elif f['operation'] == 'sum':\n   722\t            total = [q for p in parts_p for q in p[1]]\n   723\t            ok = len(total) == len(set(total)) and set(total) == out[1]\n   724\t        else:\n   725\t            ok = parts_p[1][1] <= parts_p[0][1] and parts_p[0][1] - parts_p[1][1] == out[1]\n   726\t        if not ok:\n   727\t            sign = ' + ' if f['operation'] == 'sum' else ' − '\n   728\t            errors.append(('S8', f'{fid}: {sign.join(periods[:-1])} does not make up {periods[-1]}'))\n   729\t    return errors\n   730\t\n   731\t\n   732\tdef _recorded(family, figs, outcomes):\n   733\t    recorded = []\n   734\t    scopes = family.get('part_scopes', [])\n   735\t    for f in figs.values():\n   736\t        fid = f['id']\n   737\t        if f['kind'] == 'estimate':\n   738\t            recorded.append({'type': 'estimate', 'figure': fid, 'assumption': f.get('basis_text')})\n   739\t            rested = {figs[r]['what']['scope'] for r in f.get('rests_on') or [] if r in figs}\n   740\t            if any({ps['part'], ps['whole']} <= rested for ps in scopes):\n   741\t                recorded.append({'type': 'part and whole in one estimate', 'figure': fid})\n   742\t        elif f['kind'] == 'computed':\n   743\t            money = [figs[i] for i in f['inputs'] if figs[i]['unit'] == 'USD bn']\n   744\t            if any(_quantity(i['what']) != _quantity(f['what']) for i in money) and f['operation'] != 'share':\n   745\t                recorded.append({'type': 'conversion between quantities', 'figure': fid})\n   746\t            if f['operation'] in ('apply_growth', 'product') and any(i['what']['period'] != f['what']['period'] for i in money):\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_29","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc 'nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/small_cases.py | tail -n 65'","aggregated_output":"   594\tdef _():\n   595\t    figs = [F('B', W('segment', 'FY2027', measure='share'), 'source', 'reported', '%', low=72.0, high=73.0, evidence_id='e20',\n   596\t              quote_number='between 72% and 73%', qualifier='none'),\n   597\t            F('C', W('fund', 'FY2027', measure='investment'), 'source', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=10.0, evidence_id='e21',\n   598\t              quote_number='$10 billion', qualifier='as much as'),\n   599\t            F('O', W('fund', 'FY2027', measure='investment'), 'estimate', 'forecast', 'USD bn', value=8.0, basis_text='below the cap',\n   600\t              rests_on=['B', 'C'], outcome_for=['s'])]\n   601\t    return one(figs)\n   602\t\n   603\t\n   604\t# ---------- A3: Luna's observed mistakes, encoded in v13s ----------\n   605\tdef luna(name, expect, mutate, base=base_pair, family=PAIR):\n   606\t    @case('A3', name, expect)\n   607\t    def _():\n   608\t        a = base()\n   609\t        mutate(a)\n   610\t        return a, family\n   611\t\n   612\t\n   613\tluna('a prose outcome (an outcome marker must name a member)', 'schema', lambda a: fig(a, 'F6').update(outcome_for=['$398.1 billion']))\n   614\tluna('a prose evidence id', 'schema', lambda a: fig(a, 'F1').update(evidence_id='NVIDIA reported FY2026 revenue of $215.9 billion.'))\n   615\tluna('a prose reference (a reference marker must name a member)', 'schema', lambda a: fig(a, 'F13').update(reference_for=['Analyst consensus of $411 billion']))\n   616\tluna('a unit spelled \"USD billions\"', 'schema', lambda a: fig(a, 'F1').update(unit='USD billions'))\n   617\tluna('value with a range (both given)', 'S7', lambda a: fig(a, 'F3').update(low=105.84, high=110.16))\n   618\tluna('a four-input difference', 'S7', lambda a: fig(a, 'F6').update(operation='difference'))\n   619\tluna('money × % through product', 'S2', lambda a: (fig(a, 'F10').update(operation='product', value=10175.0),\n   620\t                                                  fig(a, 'F12').update(value=10445.675)))      # arithmetic consistent: only the unit rule refuses it\n   621\tluna('\"FY2027 first half\" summed from quarters', 'S8', lambda a: a['figures'].append(F(\n   622\t    'H1', W('company', 'FY2027 first half'), 'computed', 'reported', 'USD bn', value=177.8, inputs=['F1', 'F2'], operation='sum')))\n   623\tluna('a passage id in inputs', 'S7', lambda a: fig(a, 'F6').update(inputs=['e1', 'F2', 'F4', 'F5']))\n   624\tluna('prose in inputs', 'S7', lambda a: fig(a, 'F6').update(inputs=['Q1 revenue', 'F2', 'F4', 'F5']))\n   625\tluna('an outcome in \"USD trillion\"', 'schema', lambda a: fig(a, 'F6').update(unit='USD trillion'))\n   626\tluna('\"65% growth in Q4\" copied as the number', 'S1', lambda a: a['figures'].append(F(\n   627\t    'G', W('company', 'FY2027 Q4', measure='growth'), 'source', 'third_party', '%', value=65.0, evidence_id='e8', quote_number='65% growth in Q4',\n   628\t    qualifier='none')))\n   629\t\n   630\t\n   631\tdef main():\n   632\t    failures = 0\n   633\t    for group, name, expect, recorded, build in CASES:\n   634\t        answer, family = build()\n   635\t        result = run(copy.deepcopy(answer), family)\n   636\t        if 'schema' in result:\n   637\t            got = 'schema'\n   638\t            detail = result['schema'][:1]\n   639\t        else:\n   640\t            codes = sorted({c for c, _ in result['errors']})\n   641\t            got = 'pass' if not codes else ','.join(codes)\n   642\t            detail = result['errors'][:2]\n   643\t        ok = got == expect                               # [54] exactly the registered rule(s): no other refusal may stand in for it\n   644\t        if ok and name in MESSAGES and not any(MESSAGES[name] in d for _, d in result.get('errors', [])):\n   645\t            ok, detail = False, [f'the refusal does not say \"{MESSAGES[name]}\"'] + list(result.get('errors', []))[:2]\n   646\t        if ok and recorded:\n   647\t            ok = any(r['type'] == recorded for r in result.get('recorded', []))\n   648\t            if not ok:\n   649\t                detail = [f'missing recorded {recorded}: {[r[\"type\"] for r in result.get(\"recorded\", [])]}']\n   650\t        failures += not ok\n   651\t        print(f'{\"PASS\" if ok else \"FAIL\"}  {group}  {name}  [expected {expect}{\" + recorded \" + recorded if recorded else \"\"}; got {got}]'\n   652\t              + ('' if ok else f'  {json.dumps(detail, ensure_ascii=False)[:400]}'))\n   653\t    print(f'{len(CASES) - failures} of {len(CASES)} cases as registered')\n   654\t    return 1 if failures else 0\n   655\t\n   656\t\n   657\tif __name__ == '__main__':\n   658\t    raise SystemExit(main())\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md | sed -n '38,72p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r56.md | sed -n '17,72p'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_30","type":"command_execution","command":"/opt/homebrew/bin/zsh -lc \"nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/BLIND2-RESULT.md | sed -n '38,72p'; nl -ba docs/research/2026-11-22-number-answers/claude-r56.md | sed -n '17,72p'\"","aggregated_output":"    38\t  - No v8 reference carries a past quarter's share (the S5 [D741] check refused none).\n    39\t  - D740's \"from … to …\" fix is exercised by E's Claude writer, who copied the CPU market figures without a range refusal (E's Codex\n    40\t    writer did not use them).\n    41\t\n    42\t## 2. The refusal: mechanism and census\n    43\t\n    44\t- **The mechanism.** S4's bypass walk starts at each outcome and follows `inputs`. It records every figure whose key is a listed shared\n    45\t  quantity, and it descends through every non-forecast figure, rates included. F9's unit is %, yet the walk descended into F9's inputs\n    46\t  and reached F4. A rate computed from a quarter's guidance is another quantity. The quarter's value in the calculation is still F8\n    47\t  alone.\n    48\t- **The ruling it contradicts.** D741 ruling 1 (Alex: \"不算错\"): a growth rate computed from guidance and used as a benchmark is not a\n    49\t  second value when one figure supplies the quarter in the revenue calculation. The question Alex answered named the deciding point:\n    50\t  \"进入收入计算的三季度数只有 1136 亿这一个\". The system text also asks the writer to give \"a run-rate that carries the guided growth\n    51\t  forward\". The schema's benchmark sentence names \"an estimate\", and that is the narrower wording the code follows.\n    52\t- **The census, $0** (`s4_census.py`). Every stored v13s first reply from both blind tests (24) goes through the current checker and\n    53\t  through a candidate copy.\n    54\t  - **A blanket candidate, rejected by Council round 56.** It stopped the walk beneath any % or x figure\n    55\t    (`s4-ratestop-calc_small.py.txt`). It changed 1 of 24 and passed the 104 cases. However, it also publishes a reply that builds Q4\n    56\t    on the guided $108B through a multiple applied twice to Q2 (96.2 × (108/96.2)²) while Q3 = $112.5B stands in the sum. That is a\n    57\t    second Q3 value, and the current checker refuses it.\n    58\t  - **The narrowed candidate (round 56b).** At an `apply_growth` whose base is the quarter's forecast, or is computed from it, the walk\n    59\t    skips the rate. Every other path is walked as before.\n    60\t    - 1 of 24 changes: this refusal, which becomes publish.\n    61\t    - The round-56 counterexample on the saved reply stays refused.\n    62\t    - Results on the scratch copy: 109 of 109 cases (5 new), 43 of 43 control and mutants (2 new), 19 of 19 rehearsal.\n    63\t\n    64\t## 3. Readers and adjudicators (`TALLY.json`)\n    65\t\n    66\tEach reply had a Claude reader and a Codex reader. Six replies drew a disagreement. Two adjudicators, one per family, settled each point\n    67\tagainst the passages and the format rules (the D741 rulings included). A point counts as settled when both agree.\n    68\t\n    69\t**Settled as not defects (both adjudicators reject):**\n    70\t- every \"plus or minus 2%\" guidance given as its midpoint (R06 F7, R09 F4, R10 F3). The format permits this.\n    71\t- R11 F4 (\"up to\" stands before the $3B total in e1919's quote, not before the $1B tranche).\n    72\t- R11 F12 (42 + 25 = 67 with \"more than\" kept on the source).\n    17\t- the header says \"Shared quantities … through which every figure an outcome uses for that quantity goes\".\n    18\tBoth refusal reviewers read the header as stating the refusal, and both judged the answer right. So the rule as written refuses a right\n    19\tanswer. The request and code should move together.\n    20\t\n    21\t## The proposed change (one lever)\n    22\t1. Code: S4's walk does not descend below a figure whose unit is % or x. A % or x figure carrying the shared key itself is still\n    23\t   reached.\n    24\t2. Request: one sentence aligned with D741 ruling 1. The schema's benchmark sentence becomes \"A growth rate computed from guidance may\n    25\t   be applied to your forecast or serve as a stated benchmark for an estimate; the quarter's value in the calculation stays your one\n    26\t   forecast figure.\" The header line becomes \"… through which every value of that quantity an outcome uses goes (a growth rate\n    27\t   computed from it is another quantity).\"\n    28\t3. Tests: an A1 case (a guidance-implied rate applied directly to the forecast passes); an A2 case (that rate applied to Q2 to rebuild\n    29\t   a Q3 value used beside the forecast is still S4); a mutant that removes the stop and must fail the A1 case.\n    30\t\n    31\tRisk I see: a writer can rebuild the guided Q3 under a non-shared label through a multiple (product(Q2, ratio(G, Q2))) and use it as\n    32\tanother quantity. That is relabelling, which S4 never caught when the label differs. The fix does not widen that hole for a USD bn\n    33\tfigure keyed as the shared quarter.\n    34\t\n    35\t## The two reader splits\n    36\t- **O's \"$18B\".** This is a source reading, and it decides O's rung. Ruling 2 settles R07's reference claim by its letter. The rest\n    37\t  (\"two forecasts\" for a part labelled as the whole; R11 supported) is a family threshold. The council-loop trap says boundary splits\n    38\t  go to Alex. My lean: report it to Alex as a disclosed ambiguity beside the request for the paid re-send, not as a blocking question.\n    39\t  The answers state the reading as their assumption, which is what D737 wanted readers to see.\n    40\t- **Prose numbers.** By the registered definitions they are not defects. They are, however, exactly \"hallucinated numbers\" in what an\n    41\t  analyst reads. This is outside D737's code scope. A fix would be a new mechanism (for example a prose-to-figure check), and it is\n    42\t  Alex's product call, not a v13s blocker. My lean: count them in the paid re-send as a secondary reader line, and tell Alex.\n    43\t\n    44\t## The next step (one recommendation)\n    45\tBuild the change with its cases and mutant, $0. Re-check all 24 stored replies and the rehearsal. Then run one fresh blind test\n    46\t(12 writers, $0), because the request bytes change and only a send shows how writers read the new sentence. If it is clean, take it to\n    47\tAlex as the request for the paid re-send, with the two disclosures.\n    48\t\n    49\tThe alternative is to go to Alex now with \"12 of 12 accepted on the same replies after the fix\". That judges a fixed checker on the\n    50\ttest that found the error, and the changed sentence would be untested.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
